# EMBERDEEP — Act IV: The Ember Forge (floors 13-16). Party: Wren, Brannoc, Maelis, Pip.

=== floor_13_enter
!music Forge
@narrator: The Ember Forge. Halls big enough for giants, carved by dwarves who liked to show off, and hung with chains as thick as a man. Old slag glows dull red in its channels, and the air tastes of iron and ash.
@narrator: Great anvils stand cold under the dust. Every door here was built for dwarves, and every one of them has been broken outward.
@brannoc: Here.
@wren: Brannoc?
@brannoc: This hall. We made camp here. Twelve years back. That's Kell's pipe, on the bench.
@brannoc: The Order paid us to relight the Forge. Never said what was down here. Three of mine didn't come up. Kell. Durra. Fisk.
@brannoc: Every Ironvein wears a tag. Iron, name stamped in. If they're down here, if any of it's left, I'd bring their names home. Just their names.
@wren: Then we find them. All three.
@pip: I'm very good at finding. I found a whole beetle once.
!quest start crew_tags

=== floor_14_enter
@narrator: Figures of ash shuffle through the halls, grey and crumbling, still going through the motions of their work: hammer, lift, carry, hammer.
@maelis: Ash thralls. The Forge's fire took them before the Pale did. They don't know they've stopped.
@brannoc: That one. The broad one. That's an Ironvein belt.
@wren: Brannoc, you don't have to —
@brannoc: I do. They'd want it quick, and done by someone who knew their names.
@pip: I'll hold your hat, Big-beard.

=== floor_15_enter
@narrator: The halls narrow toward a great sealed gate. Scratched into the soot, in letters no more than a year old: an arrow pointing down, and one word. ONWARD.
@wren: That's Ilsa's hand. She wrote "onward" on the lamp roster every time she finished a street.
@brannoc: She got past it, then. The thing below. The iron one.
@maelis: The ledger said "Hadric Moll, bound in iron at the Forge". You went still when Wren read that name, Brannoc.
@brannoc: Knew a Hadric. Sixty years back. A lad. Used to watch me work, wanted to be a miner. They made him Warden instead.
@brannoc: Carried his pack to the Vaultgate myself. Told him he was the bravest lad in Hollowmere.
@pip: Was he?
@brannoc: Yes.

=== floor_16_enter
@narrator: The Warden's Hall: a forge as big as a temple. At its heart burns a single fire, blue-white, fed by nothing anyone can see.
@narrator: Before it stands a figure in iron, twice a man's height. Chains run from its armour into the walls, the floor, the vaulted roof. It has not moved in a very long time.
@pip: Is it sleeping?
@brannoc: No. Waiting. Saw it once, twelve years ago, from the other side of that fire.
@wren: Brannoc, if you'd rather stay back —
@brannoc: Not this time.

=== iron_warden_pre
!music Boss
@narrator: As they come near, the iron figure lifts its head. Deep inside the helm, two cold lights kindle.
@hadric: Turn back. The Forge is shut. By order of the Lantern. Turn back. Turn back.
@brannoc: Hadric.
@narrator: The chains grind. The iron head turns toward the dwarf, slow as a millstone.
@hadric: You... the crew... I... TURN BACK.
@brannoc: It's him. Stone and ember. It was him all along.
@wren: Brannoc —
@brannoc: Put him down, lass. Gently as we can. He's been standing there sixty years.
!sfx BossRoar
!shake
!battle iron_warden

=== iron_warden_post
!music Story
@narrator: The iron shell splits from helm to heel. The chains fall slack one by one, with a sound like temple bells.
@narrator: Inside is an old man, thin as kindling, blinking in the firelight as though waking from a long fever.
@hadric: Brannoc? Is that you? You got old.
@brannoc: You got iron.
@hadric: They did that. The Order's smiths. I told them I wouldn't burn, so they said I'd guard instead. And the iron gets into your head.
@hadric: Your crew. I remember now. They came with picks and lanterns, and I couldn't stop the iron. Kell sang while he worked. I remember that.
@brannoc: He did. Terrible voice.
@brannoc: The Order put you in that iron, lad. Then it sent us down to mend your chains and never said what was in them. I forgive you. Hear me? I forgive you.
@hadric: Lamplighter. A year ago a woman came through with a lantern, and she talked to me. Nobody had talked to me in sixty years.
@hadric: She said there had to be another way. I believed her. The iron let me stand aside, just once.
@hadric: She had your eyes.
@wren: She's my sister. Ilsa. Was she all right?
@hadric: She was cold. Colder than me. But she was singing. That old tune, the one with the lantern in it.
@hadric: The grief at the bottom has a name, lamplighter. It's forgotten it. She went down to remind it.
@narrator: Hadric closes his eyes. The cold lights behind them go out, gently, the way a lamp does at dawn.
@brannoc: Rest now, lad. Rest.
